/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaScriptura.Canon — how the canon is recognised

The canon objection asks who told us which books are Scripture. Augustine wrote
against the Manichaeans that he would not believe the gospel unless the
authority of the Catholic Church moved him to (*Against the Epistle of
Manichaeus* 5.6), and Rome has cited him since. Calvin read the same sentence
as Augustine describing how an unbeliever is first brought to the gospel, not
where the believer's certainty rests (*Institutes* I.vii.3). If the Church's
authority is what identifies Scripture, and that identification needs an
infallible authority outside Scripture, then Scripture is not the only
infallible rule. The chapter
"Three objections, and the answers to them" gives Kruger's parity reply, which
grants the Church's reception and answers that every final authority vouches
for itself. That reply stops the objection from proving its point, but proves
nothing for the Protestant side either. The reply in this chapter goes further,
and denies that identifying the canon needs an infallible authority outside
Scripture at all.

## Tests Scripture sets

Israel had to tell God's word from a counterfeit long before there was a
Church. Moses gave two tests. The first is agreement with what God has already
said: "If a prophet or a dreamer of dreams arises among you and gives you a sign
or a wonder, and the sign or the wonder comes true, concerning which he spoke to
you, saying, 'Let us walk after other gods (whom you have not known) and let us
serve them,' you shall not listen to the words of that prophet" (Deuteronomy
13:1–3). The second is whether the word comes true. "But the prophet who speaks
a word presumptuously in My name which I have not commanded him to speak, or
which he speaks in the name of other gods, that prophet shall die. Now you may
say in your heart, 'How will we know the word which Yahweh has not spoken?' When
a prophet speaks in the name of Yahweh, if the thing does not come about or come
true, that is the thing which Yahweh has not spoken" (18:20–22). Both tests give
the hearer something to check for himself, what the prophet says and whether it
comes true, and neither sends him to an office that cannot err.

## Scripture decides what can be Scripture

The New Testament applies the same tests to itself. Paul tells the Ephesians
that they have been "built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets,
Christ Jesus Himself being the corner stone" (Ephesians 2:20), and Jude writes
of "the faith which was once for all handed down to the saints" (Jude 3). Peter
counts an apostle's letters as Scripture. He writes that "our beloved brother
Paul ... wrote to you, as also in all his letters", in which are things "which
the untaught and unstable distort, as they do also the rest of the Scriptures,
to their own destruction" (2 Peter 3:15–16). Earlier in the same chapter he sets
"the commandment of the Lord and Savior spoken by your apostles" beside "the
words spoken beforehand by the holy prophets" (3:2). In 1 Timothy 5:18, "the
Scripture says" introduces a line of Deuteronomy and then "The laborer is worthy
of his wages", a saying of Jesus that Luke 10:7 records in the same words
(μισθός ("wage"), "wages", in both).

Being an apostle is not enough by itself. Paul holds even himself to the
gospel already given: "But even if we, or an angel from heaven, should proclaim
to you a gospel contrary to the gospel we have proclaimed to you, let him be
accursed!" (Galatians 1:8). That is Deuteronomy 13 carried forward. A sign does not
outweigh a message at odds with what God has said, and neither does an office.
So for the New Testament the test has two parts: whether a book comes from an
apostle, or from the apostles' circle as Mark, Luke and Hebrews were received,
and whether it agrees with the Scripture already given.

A book written after the apostles cannot pass the first part, however sound its
teaching. The same holds for the books written between the Testaments. Josephus,
writing at the end of the first century, says the Jews have "only twenty-two
books ... which are justly believed to be divine", and that what was written
after the Persian king Artaxerxes "hath not been esteemed of the like authority
with the former by our forefathers, because there hath not been an exact
succession of prophets since that time" (*Against Apion* I.8, in Whiston's
translation). 1 Maccabees says the same of its own day, a distress "such as had
not been since the time a prophet had last appeared among them" (9:27, NRSVUE). This is
the ground on which Protestants leave out the Apocrypha, the books Trent
receives and the Reformers read but do not count as Scripture, and 1 Enoch.
Jude quotes Enoch: "Behold, the Lord came with many thousands of His holy ones"
(Jude 14). Quoting a book is not receiving it, though, since Paul quotes Greek
poets to the Athenians (Acts 17:28).

## Under providence

None of this is a committee's checklist. The Westminster Confession says the
Old Testament in Hebrew and the New in Greek were "immediately inspired by God,
and by his singular care and providence kept pure in all ages" (I.8). The tests
are how the church recognises what God has kept and given. They are applied
under that providence and do not take its place, which is why the reply
counts it among its grounds.

## Books the Church read and did not receive

The early Church drew a line between books it read and books it received as
Scripture, and it said why. The Muratorian fragment, an early list of the New
Testament books whose date is disputed (the late second century or the fourth),
says that Hermas wrote the Shepherd (the *Pastor*, in the translation quoted)
"very recently in our times in the city of Rome ... And therefore it also ought
to be read; but it cannot be made public in the Church to the people, nor
placed among the prophets, as their number is complete, nor among the apostles
to the end of time". The fragment gives a reason only for the prophets. Read
with "very recently in our times", its exclusion from the apostles is a
judgement about when the book was written. Athanasius, in his Festal Letter of 367,
lists the books of both Testaments and calls them "fountains of salvation ...
Let no man add to these". He then names "other books besides these not indeed
included in the Canon, but appointed by the Fathers to be read by those who
newly join us": the Wisdom of Solomon, Sirach, Esther, Judith and Tobit, "and
that which is called the Teaching of the Apostles" (the Didache) "and the
Shepherd". The line he drew is not exactly the one either Trent or the
Reformers drew, since Trent receives Wisdom, Sirach, Judith and Tobit and the
Reformers receive Esther. The reasons these witnesses give are ones a reader
can weigh: when a book was written, whether it came from the apostles' time,
what had been handed down. The last of these is a reason from the Church's
reception, which is the ground Rome stands on.

So the reply runs. Scripture itself gives tests for a word that claims to come
from God: agreement with what he has already said, and, for the New Testament,
an apostle's authority. The Church set books apart by those reasons, and God by
his providence keeps his word while it does. So identifying the canon is a
matter of recognising what God gave, and needs no infallible authority outside
Scripture to do it.

## Rome's answer

Both sides agree that the Church recognises the canon and does not make it.
The First Vatican Council says the Church holds the books "sacred and canonical
not because she subsequently approved them by her authority after they had been
composed by unaided human skill, nor simply because they contain revelation
without error, but because, being written under the inspiration of the holy
Spirit, they have God as their author" (*Dei Filius*, ch. 2). What divides them
is whether the recognition must come from an infallible authority outside the
books.

George Reid, writing in the *Catholic Encyclopedia* in 1908, grants that
apostolic origin is a real test, tells how the Shepherd and the Didache were
set apart under Athanasius, and as a Catholic does not dispute what
Deuteronomy says. He denies that such tests reach far enough. He calls
apostolic origin "a positive and partial touchstone of inspiration", and then
answers the reply directly. Inspiration "is not self-evident", and so "Its sole
absolute criterion, therefore, is the Holy inspiring Spirit, witnessing
decisively to Itself, not in the subjective experience of individual souls, as
Calvin maintained, neither in the doctrinal and spiritual tenor of Holy Writ
itself, according to Luther, but through the constituted organ and custodian of
Its revelations, the Church. All other evidences fall short of the certainty and
finality necessary to compel the absolute assent of faith." Augustine's rule,
which Reid follows, is reception rather than agreement with earlier Scripture:
prefer the books "received by all the Catholic churches" (*On Christian
Doctrine* II.8.12). Because Reid grants the reply's grounds and denies its
conclusion, the step from one to the other is rated `disputed`.

## Two Protestant cautions

Michael Kruger agrees with the reply's conclusion and with much of its route.
His answer is that the canon authenticates itself: Christ speaks in the books by
his Spirit, as Calvin rests the certainty of Scripture on "the secret testimony
of the Spirit" (*Institutes* I.vii.4). His own model counts God's providential
exposure of the books to the church and their apostolic origin among its parts,
with the Spirit's witness (*Canon Revisited*, the chapter "My sheep hear my
voice"). What he faults is the appeal to tests taken on their own, while
granting that it has "rightly focused on apostolicity as a key component of
canonicity", that is, on whether a book comes from an apostle or his circle
(pp. 67–87). Sound doctrine alone cannot pick out the canon, because the
Shepherd taught sound doctrine too. That is why the reply joins agreement with
the earlier Scripture to apostolic origin, and both to providence.

Lee Martin McDonald doubts that tests settled the list at all. The Fathers, he
writes, "employed several criteria, often unequally", and the care with which
they applied them "is, in several instances, highly questionable". Not everyone
drew the line in the same place: Irenaeus quotes the Shepherd as "the
Scripture". Moses' tests can show that a word is not from God. By themselves
they cannot show that a book belongs in the canon.

## What the checks show

Granted its eight grounds and its step from them, the reply reaches what it
claims: identifying the canon does not need an infallible authority outside
Scripture (`canon_tests_deny_the_ground`). The step is the part in dispute. Nor
does the reply, on its own, deliver sola scriptura: with the tests granted,
Scripture may be the sole infallible rule or not, and the reply does not choose
(`canon_tests_leave_the_sole_rule_open`).

Weighed against each other, each defeats the other. The reply denies a claim
the objection rests on, and the objection, by holding that claim, denies what
the reply concludes. Each rests at its weakest on a link rated `disputed`, its
lowest-rated premise or step. For the objection that is the claim that
identifying the canon needs an infallible authority outside Scripture, held by
Trent and Reid and denied by Calvin and Kruger. For the reply it is its step,
which Reid denies. No rating breaks the tie, so the dispute forces neither: no
fair weighing of the two must accept one and reject the other
(`canon_dispute_forces_nothing`). Each can be held, because each answers its
one attacker (`canon_tests_defensible`, `canon_objection_defensible`). The
reply answers the objection but does not overthrow it, and the objection does
not overthrow the reply. The verdict would move only with a new, cited reason
to rate one of those two links above `disputed`.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaScriptura

open Testimony

open Testimony Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework

/-! #### How the reply is built

Kruger's parity reply only declines to grant that identifying the canon needs
an infallible authority outside Scripture. This reply asserts the opposite, so
it is a line of its own with its own step, delivering the negation of that
claim. Asserting the opposite is an attack, so the reply and the objection can
be weighed against each other in a dispute (the section "Refusing a ground, and
denying it" in "Three objections, and the answers to them"). -/

/-- If Scripture tests a word that claims to come from God, by what it says
and by whether it comes to pass; if it builds the church on the apostles and
counts their letters as Scripture, and holds even an apostle to the gospel
first given; if the books written after prophecy ceased, and those after the
apostles, were read but not received; and if God keeps his word by his
providence while the tests are applied; then identifying the canon does not
require an infallible authority outside Scripture. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonTestsStep : Formula Claim :=
  ⋀ [ p .deut13_1_3ProphetOfOtherGods
    , p .deut18_20_22WordNotFulfilled
    , p .eph2_20FoundationOfApostles
    , p .pet3_15_16PaulAmongScriptures
    , p .gal1_8NoOtherGospel
    , p .prophecyCeasedBeforeLaterBooks
    , p .booksReadButNotReceived
    , p .providenceKeepsScripture ] ➝ notP .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority

/-- **The canon recognised by Scripture's tests.** -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonTestsLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The canon recognised by Scripture's tests"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .deut13_1_3ProphetOfOtherGods
      , p .deut18_20_22WordNotFulfilled
      , p .eph2_20FoundationOfApostles
      , p .pet3_15_16PaulAmongScriptures
      , p .gal1_8NoOtherGospel
      , p .prophecyCeasedBeforeLaterBooks
      , p .booksReadButNotReceived
      , p .providenceKeepsScripture ]
  , step := canonTestsStep
  , delivers := notP .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
  , inference := some canonTestsInference }

/-- The reply as an argument of its own. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonTestsReply : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  canonTestsLine.asPackage cite
    "identifying the canon does not require an infallible authority outside Scripture"

/-! #### What it shows -/

/-- **The reply denies the canon objection's ground.** Granted Moses' two tests
(Deuteronomy 13:1–3 and 18:20–22), the apostles as the church's foundation
(Ephesians 2:20) and their letters counted as Scripture (2 Peter 3:15–16),
the apostles' gospel as the test of any later one (Galatians 1:8), the end of
prophecy before the later Jewish books, the early Church's setting apart of
books it read but did not receive, God's providence over his word, and the
step from these to the conclusion, identifying the canon does not require an
infallible authority outside Scripture.

What this does not claim: that the eight grounds are enough without the step.
The step is rated `disputed`, because Reid grants the grounds and denies the
conclusion. -/
@[headline]
theorem canon_tests_deny_the_ground : Establishes canonTestsReply := by
  establish [solaScripturaDefs]

#print axioms canon_tests_deny_the_ground

/-- The reply's premises have a model: the reading on which every claim holds
except that identifying the canon needs an infallible authority outside
Scripture (`krugerParityReading`, the reading Kruger's own view gives). -/
theorem canonTestsReply_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable canonTestsReply.premises := by
  satisfied_by krugerParityReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The reading on which the tests are granted, no infallible authority is
needed outside Scripture to identify the canon, and Scripture is still not the
sole infallible rule. -/
def testsWithoutSoleRuleReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority => False
  | .scriptureIsSoleInfallibleRule => False
  | _ => True

/-- **The reply leaves the sole rule open.** Granted the reply's grounds,
Scripture may be the sole infallible rule or not: Kruger's reading has it, and
a reading that grants the tests and still denies the sole rule has it not. Both
satisfy every premise.

So the reply clears a ground the objection needed, and establishes nothing more
by itself. The Protestant position's own lines carry the case for sola
scriptura; they are set out in "Three objections, and the answers to them". -/
@[headline]
theorem canon_tests_leave_the_sole_rule_open :
    Independent canonTestsLine.premises (p .scriptureIsSoleInfallibleRule) := by
  leaves_open testsWithoutSoleRuleReading krugerParityReading [solaScripturaDefs]

#print axioms canon_tests_leave_the_sole_rule_open

/-! ### The dispute -/

/-- The parties to the dispute over the canon. -/
inductive CanonParty
  /-- The canon objection. -/
  | objection
  /-- The reply from Scripture's tests. -/
  | tests
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The package each party argues from. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonPartyNode : CanonParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .objection => canonObjection
  | .tests => canonTestsReply

/-- The dispute over the canon: the objection, and the reply from Scripture's
tests. Each side's premises can all be true together, each side's conclusion
follows from its premises, and every step is rated. -/
declare_dispute canonDispute := canonPartyNode using solaScripturaDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom**, all four pairs. The objection and the reply defeat
  each other, and nothing else defeats anything. The reply denies the
  objection's claim that identifying the canon needs an infallible authority
  outside Scripture, and the objection, holding that claim, denies what the
  reply concludes. Both rest at their weakest on a link rated `disputed`, so
  neither attack is outweighed.

  Every cell is computed from the two sides' premises and checked by Lean's
  kernel (`Horn.defeats?`). -/
  defeats [(.objection, .tests), (.tests, .objection)]
  /-- Neither side lends the other a premise (`supports?`, checked by the
  kernel). -/
  supports []
  /-- Neither side's case contains the other's (`partOf?`, checked by the
  kernel). -/
  partOf []
  /-- The dispute over the canon drawn: the objection and the reply, each
  defeating the other. -/
  map canonMap

/-! ### What the dispute decides -/

/-- Why nothing is forced: the objection has the reply as a defeater, and the
reply has the objection. -/
declare_verdict canonSettlesNothing for canonDispute :
    .nothingGrounded [(.objection, .tests), (.tests, .objection)]
  /-- **The dispute over the canon forces neither side.** The objection and the
  reply are in a standoff, and both rest on a link rated `disputed`, so no
  rating breaks the tie.

  What this does not claim: that the two are equally good arguments. It claims
  that, on the ratings the sources support, neither is shown to outweigh the
  other. -/
  proves @[headline] canon_dispute_forces_nothing

#print axioms canon_dispute_forces_nothing

/-- Why the reply can be defended: it answers its only attacker, the objection,
without help from any other party. -/
declare_verdict canonTestsStandAlone for canonDispute :
    .credulous .tests [.tests]
  /-- **The reply from Scripture's tests can be defended.** Someone can hold
  it, because the one party that defeats it, the objection, it defeats back. So,
  on the ratings given, the canon objection does not force the conclusion that
  Scripture is not the sole rule. By the same verdict the reply does not force
  the objection out (`canon_objection_defensible`). -/
  proves @[headline] canon_tests_defensible

#print axioms canon_tests_defensible

/-- Why the objection can be defended: it answers its only attacker, the reply,
without help from any other party. -/
declare_verdict canonObjectionStandsAlone for canonDispute :
    .credulous .objection [.objection]
  /-- **The canon objection can be defended too.** Someone can hold it, because
  it defeats the reply back. Read with the verdict before it: each
  side has an answer to the other, and the dispute does not choose. -/
  proves @[headline] canon_objection_defensible

#print axioms canon_objection_defensible

/-! ## How Rome says the canon is known

Reid's answer to the reply is that only the Church gives the certainty faith
needs about which books are canonical. A Protestant reader will ask the next
question: how is the Church's own authority known, so that it can give that
certainty? Catholic writers answer it in more than one way, and each answer
meets a different reply. So the objection's claim, that identifying the canon
requires an infallible authority outside Scripture, is read four ways here,
each with who reads it so, and each set against the Protestant reply that
answers it.

The first way proves the Church from Scripture received as inspired. William
Whitaker, the Cambridge divine who answered Bellarmine in 1588, asks "whence do
we know" that the Church cannot err in setting the canon, and answers that Rome
must prove it from Scripture: "Surely it is a notable circle in which this
argument revolves!" (*A Disputation on Holy Scripture*, pp. 334–335). The
Catholic writers who state the second way grant the point. "That indeed would
be a circular argument!", says the Catholic Answers tract "Proving Inspiration".

The second way is the one that tract calls "really a spiral argument". Patrick
Toner gives it in the *Catholic Encyclopedia* (1910): Scripture is used
"merely as reliable historical sources" to show that Christ gave the Church
infallible authority, and then "it is no vicious circle, but a perfectly
legitimate logical procedure, to rely on the Church's authority for proof of
what writings are inspired". William Chillingworth answered this kind of
argument in 1638. Certainty reached by reasoning, he wrote, cannot "by natural
means be more certain ... than of the weaker of the premises; as a river will
not rise higher than the fountain from which it flows" (*The Religion of
Protestants*, p. 66). Turretin says the same of the Church: it can be grasped
"by human faith before Scripture", but not "known and believed ... by divine
faith" until Scripture is known. The First Vatican Council denies that the
history is so weak, condemning the claim that miracles "can never be known with
certainty"; and it holds that faith believes "because of the authority of God
himself", by grace, and not because of the arguments that make it credible
(*Dei Filius*, ch. 3).

The third way is the Church's own marks. Vatican I calls the Church, "by
reason of her astonishing propagation, her outstanding holiness ... her
catholic unity and her unconquerable stability", "a kind of great and
perpetual motive of credibility" (ch. 3). Robert Bellarmine, the Jesuit
cardinal whom Whitaker answered, grants that the marks do not make it evident
that she is the true Church, only "evidently credible", since otherwise no one
would deny it (*De Ecclesia militante* IV.3). Calvin adds that the Greeks claim
the same unbroken succession of bishops (*Institutes* IV.ii.2), and Turretin
that such marks are "common to other bodies" and "confuse rather than
distinguish". Bellarmine still holds the Church's word certain, as Vatican I
does, because faith rests on God's authority with "the effective help of power
from on high".

The fourth way needs no definition at all. Reid's own criterion is "the
universal reception of compositions as Divinely inspired, or the ordinary
teaching of the Church". The reply is that the reception was not universal for
the books in question. Jerome wrote that "the Church reads Judith, Tobit, and
the books of Maccabees, but does not admit them among the canonical
Scriptures". Gregory the Great cited 1 Maccabees as a book "though not
Canonical, yet brought out for the edifying of the Church" (*Morals on Job*
XIX.xxi.34). And Cardinal Cajetan, a few years before Trent, followed Jerome.
Reid grants all of this: "in the Latin Church, all through the Middle Ages we
find evidence of hesitation about the character of the deuterocanonicals", and
Trent's decree "was the first infallible and effectually promulgated
pronouncement on the Canon". He answers that "these doubts must be regarded as
more or less academic", because "Ecclesiastical usage and Roman tradition held
firmly".

So each reading falls to its reply, and the page says at what rating. The
first falls at a step no one was found to dispute, because the Catholic writers
leave that route themselves. The other three fall only at steps rated
`disputed`, and the reason is the same each time: faith's certainty, Rome
holds, comes from God's authority, believed by grace through the Church, and
not from the arguments that lead to her. Against the marks and against
reception Rome grants the reply's ground and answers only this. Against the
spiral it also denies the ground, since Vatican I holds the history can be
known with certainty. That is where the question now rests. -/

/-! #### The four replies -/

/-- If proving the Church from inspired Scripture, and inspiration from the
Church, is a circle, then that is not how the authority that identifies the
canon is known. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def circleAnswerStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .provingEachFromTheOtherIsCircular ➝ notP .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture

/-- **Whitaker's circle.** -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def circleAnswerLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Whitaker's circle"
  , grounds := [p .provingEachFromTheOtherIsCircular]
  , step := circleAnswerStep
  , delivers := notP .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture
  , inference := some circleAnswerInference }

/-- Whitaker's circle, as an argument of its own. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def circleAnswer : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  circleAnswerLine.asPackage cite
    "the canon's authority is not known by proving the Church from inspired Scripture"

/-- If a conclusion reasoned from history is no surer than that history, then
the spiral does not give the certainty of faith about the canon. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def spiralAnswerStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .conclusionNoSurerThanItsHistory ➝ notP .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon

/-- **No surer than its history.** -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def spiralAnswerLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "No surer than its history"
  , grounds := [p .conclusionNoSurerThanItsHistory]
  , step := spiralAnswerStep
  , delivers := notP .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon
  , inference := some spiralAnswerInference }

/-- Chillingworth's answer to the spiral, as an argument of its own. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def spiralAnswer : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  spiralAnswerLine.asPackage cite "the spiral does not give the certainty of faith"

/-- If the marks make the Church's claim only credible, and other churches claim
them too, then the marks do not give the certainty of faith about the canon. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def marksAnswerStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .marksMakeTheClaimOnlyCredible ➝ notP .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon

/-- **The marks make the claim only credible.** -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def marksAnswerLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The marks make the claim only credible"
  , grounds := [p .marksMakeTheClaimOnlyCredible]
  , step := marksAnswerStep
  , delivers := notP .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon
  , inference := some marksAnswerInference }

/-- The answer from the marks, as an argument of its own. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def marksAnswer : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  marksAnswerLine.asPackage cite "the Church's marks do not give the certainty of faith"

/-- If the Latin Church did not receive the disputed books with one voice before
Trent, then reception had not settled the canon. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def receptionAnswerStep : Formula Claim :=
  p .disputedBooksDoubtedBeforeTrent ➝ notP .receptionSettledTheCanon

/-- **The doubts before Trent.** -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def receptionAnswerLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The doubts before Trent"
  , grounds := [p .disputedBooksDoubtedBeforeTrent]
  , step := receptionAnswerStep
  , delivers := notP .receptionSettledTheCanon
  , inference := some receptionAnswerInference }

/-- The answer from the doubts before Trent, as an argument of its own. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def receptionAnswer : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  receptionAnswerLine.asPackage cite "reception had not settled the canon before Trent"

/-! #### Four readings of the objection's claim -/

/-- The objection's claim, **read as resting on Scripture received as
inspired**: the infallible authority is known by proving the Church from
Scripture as inspired. This is the reading Whitaker answers; the Catholic
writers who state the spiral reject it. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def asFromInspiredScripture : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as known from Scripture received as inspired"
  , commits := p .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture
  , source := (cite .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture).source }

/-- The objection's claim, **read as the spiral**: Scripture read as history
shows an infallible Church, whose word gives the certainty of faith. Toner and
Catholic Answers read it so. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def asSpiral : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as the spiral from history to the Church"
  , commits := p .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon
  , source := (cite .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon).source }

/-- The objection's claim, **read as resting on the Church's marks**, as
Vatican I describes them. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def asMarks : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as known by the Church's marks"
  , commits := p .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon
  , source := (cite .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon).source }

/-- The objection's claim, **read as reception without a definition**, Reid's
own criterion. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def asReception : Reading Claim :=
  { name := "as universal reception, with no definition"
  , commits := p .receptionSettledTheCanon
  , source := (cite .receptionSettledTheCanon).source }

/-- The objection, read as resting on Scripture received as inspired. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonObjectionFromInspiredScripture : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  canonObjection.readAs (p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority) asFromInspiredScripture

/-- The objection, read as the spiral. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonObjectionAsSpiral : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  canonObjection.readAs (p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority) asSpiral

/-- The objection, read as resting on the Church's marks. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonObjectionAsMarks : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  canonObjection.readAs (p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority) asMarks

/-- The objection, read as reception with no definition. -/
@[solaScripturaDefs]
def canonObjectionAsReception : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  canonObjection.readAs (p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority) asReception

/-! #### Readings, written down -/

/-- Rome's world: every claim holds, each of the four routes among them, except
that Scripture is the sole infallible rule. -/
def romeCanonReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .scriptureIsSoleInfallibleRule => False
  | _ => True

/-- The world of the four replies: every claim holds except the four routes to
the Church's certainty about the canon. -/
def repliesCanonReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture => False
  | .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon => False
  | .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon => False
  | .receptionSettledTheCanon => False
  | _ => True

/-! #### Each reading is fair, and each reply holds -/

/-- The objection, read as resting on inspired Scripture, can be held without
contradiction. -/
theorem canonObjectionFromInspiredScripture_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable canonObjectionFromInspiredScripture.premises := by
  satisfied_by romeCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The objection, read as the spiral, can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem canonObjectionAsSpiral_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable canonObjectionAsSpiral.premises := by
  satisfied_by romeCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The objection, read as resting on the marks, can be held without
contradiction. -/
theorem canonObjectionAsMarks_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable canonObjectionAsMarks.premises := by
  satisfied_by romeCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The objection, read as reception, can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem canonObjectionAsReception_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable canonObjectionAsReception.premises := by
  satisfied_by romeCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- Whitaker's circle delivers its conclusion. -/
theorem circleAnswer_establishes : Establishes circleAnswer := by
  establish [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- Whitaker's circle can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem circleAnswer_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable circleAnswer.premises := by
  satisfied_by repliesCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer to the spiral delivers its conclusion. -/
theorem spiralAnswer_establishes : Establishes spiralAnswer := by
  establish [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer to the spiral can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem spiralAnswer_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable spiralAnswer.premises := by
  satisfied_by repliesCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer from the marks delivers its conclusion. -/
theorem marksAnswer_establishes : Establishes marksAnswer := by
  establish [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer from the marks can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem marksAnswer_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable marksAnswer.premises := by
  satisfied_by repliesCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer from the doubts before Trent delivers its conclusion. -/
theorem receptionAnswer_establishes : Establishes receptionAnswer := by
  establish [solaScripturaDefs]

/-- The answer from the doubts before Trent can be held without
contradiction. -/
theorem receptionAnswer_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable receptionAnswer.premises := by
  satisfied_by repliesCanonReading [solaScripturaDefs]

/-! #### Where each reading breaks -/

/-- **Why the circle stands against the first reading.** The crux is Whitaker's
step: a circle is not how the authority is known. It breaks the objection's
claim together with the reading, and nothing else in the objection. The step is
rated `wellSupported`, because the Catholic writers who state the spiral grant
the circle and leave this route themselves. -/
def whereTheFirstReadingFalls : Because circleAnswer canonObjectionFromInspiredScripture :=
  Because.ofChecks circleAnswerStep
    [p .provingEachFromTheOtherIsCircular] [] [p .provingEachFromTheOtherIsCircular]
    [ p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
    , p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority ➝ p .churchKnownFromInspiredScripture ]
    .derives circleAnswer_establishes circleAnswer_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises, ArgumentPackage.readAs])
    (by decide +kernel)
  |>.ratedByLine circleAnswerLine rfl (by simp [circleAnswer, Line.asPackage])

/-- **Why the answer to the spiral stands against the second reading.** The
crux is the step from "no surer than its history" to "no certainty of faith".
It is rated `disputed`: Vatican I grants that faith does not rest on the
arguments that make it credible, and holds it certain on God's authority, by
grace. -/
def whereTheSpiralFalls : Because spiralAnswer canonObjectionAsSpiral :=
  Because.ofChecks spiralAnswerStep
    [p .conclusionNoSurerThanItsHistory] [] [p .conclusionNoSurerThanItsHistory]
    [ p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
    , p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority ➝ p .spiralGivesCertaintyOfTheCanon ]
    .derives spiralAnswer_establishes spiralAnswer_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises, ArgumentPackage.readAs])
    (by decide +kernel)
  |>.ratedByLine spiralAnswerLine rfl (by simp [spiralAnswer, Line.asPackage])

/-- **Why the answer from the marks stands against the third reading.** The crux
is the step from "only credible" to "no certainty of faith", rated `disputed`:
Bellarmine grants that the marks make the Church only "evidently credible", and
still holds her word certain. -/
def whereTheMarksFall : Because marksAnswer canonObjectionAsMarks :=
  Because.ofChecks marksAnswerStep
    [p .marksMakeTheClaimOnlyCredible] [] [p .marksMakeTheClaimOnlyCredible]
    [ p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
    , p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority ➝ p .marksGiveCertaintyOfTheCanon ]
    .derives marksAnswer_establishes marksAnswer_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises, ArgumentPackage.readAs])
    (by decide +kernel)
  |>.ratedByLine marksAnswerLine rfl (by simp [marksAnswer, Line.asPackage])

/-- **Why the doubts before Trent stand against the fourth reading.** The crux is
the step from the doubts to "reception had not settled the canon", rated
`disputed`: Reid grants the doubts and calls them "more or less academic". -/
def whereReceptionFalls : Because receptionAnswer canonObjectionAsReception :=
  Because.ofChecks receptionAnswerStep
    [p .disputedBooksDoubtedBeforeTrent] [] [p .disputedBooksDoubtedBeforeTrent]
    [ p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
    , p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority ➝ p .receptionSettledTheCanon ]
    .derives receptionAnswer_establishes receptionAnswer_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises])
    (by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises, ArgumentPackage.readAs])
    (by decide +kernel)
  |>.ratedByLine receptionAnswerLine rfl (by simp [receptionAnswer, Line.asPackage])

/-! #### The dilemma -/

/-- **How the Church's authority over the canon is known, read four ways.** The
claim is the objection's: identifying the canon requires an infallible authority
outside Scripture.

Read as resting on Scripture received as inspired, it falls to Whitaker's
circle at a step rated `wellSupported`, and the Catholic writers who state the
spiral do not hold it. Read as the spiral, as resting on the Church's marks, or
as universal reception with no definition, it falls to Chillingworth's answer,
to the answer from the marks, and to the doubts before Trent, each at a step
rated `disputed`. Against the marks and against reception, Rome grants the
reply's ground and answers that faith's certainty comes from God's authority,
by grace, through the Church. Against the spiral it gives the same answer, and
denies the ground as well, holding that the history can be known with
certainty, so that ground is rated `disputed` too.

Each reading is fair: the objection read any of these ways can be held without
contradiction. The dilemma does not say which reading Rome means; it shows what
each costs. -/
def howTheCanonIsKnown : Dilemma canonObjection where
  claim := p .identifyingCanonRequiresInfallibleAuthority
  horns :=
    [ { reading := asFromInspiredScripture
      , fair := canonObjectionFromInspiredScripture_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls circleAnswer whereTheFirstReadingFalls]
      , answered := by simp }
    , { reading := asSpiral
      , fair := canonObjectionAsSpiral_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls spiralAnswer whereTheSpiralFalls]
      , answered := by simp }
    , { reading := asMarks
      , fair := canonObjectionAsMarks_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls marksAnswer whereTheMarksFall]
      , answered := by simp }
    , { reading := asReception
      , fair := canonObjectionAsReception_is_satisfiable
      , fates := [.falls receptionAnswer whereReceptionFalls]
      , answered := by simp } ]
  claim_mem := by simp [solaScripturaDefs, Line.asPackage, Line.premises]
  two := by simp

end TestimonyArguments.SolaScriptura
